          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What blood pressure target does guidance describe for most adults?",
          "answer": "The retrieved guidance does not give a specific blood pressure target for most adults. The main passage on this, from \"Hypertension: diagnostic thresholds and blood-pressure targets\" (a major cardiology society consensus summary), says it covers \"the treatment target most bodies set once treatment has begun,\" but the text returned to me stops before naming the number.\n\nThat same document does describe how adult office readings are classified:\n\n- **Normal:** top number (systolic) below 120 and bottom number (diastolic) below 80.\n- **Elevated:** systolic 120 to 129, with diastolic below 80.\n- **Stage 1 hypertension:** systolic 130 to 139, or diastolic 80 to 89.\n- **Stage 2 hypertension:** systolic 140 or higher, or diastolic 90 or higher.\n\nIf the two numbers fall into different categories, the higher one applies.\n\nTo find the target itself, you could ask a clinician, or look up the full version of that guidance. A clinician can also say what target fits your own situation.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
